1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,846
What if a second sun rose at midnight?

2
00:00:02,886 --> 00:00:04,510
The clock chimes twelve.

3
00:00:04,550 --> 00:00:06,661
The streets are dark and quiet.

4
00:00:06,701 --> 00:00:08,325
Then, over the rooftops,

5
00:00:08,365 --> 00:00:11,169
a new glow pushes up like someone turning

6
00:00:11,209 --> 00:00:12,071
on the world.

7
00:00:13,250 --> 00:00:13,992
Birds panic.

8
00:00:14,032 --> 00:00:16,597
Roosters shout good morning at the moon.

9
00:00:16,637 --> 00:00:18,226
Flowers open in the dark,

10
00:00:18,266 --> 00:00:21,027
and sleepy people in pajamas drift outside,

11
00:00:21,067 --> 00:00:24,675
squinting at the brightest midnight they have ever seen.

12
00:00:25,150 --> 00:00:26,524
Then the town adapts.

13
00:00:26,564 --> 00:00:27,871
Picnics at midnight.

14
00:00:27,911 --> 00:00:30,295
Skateboarders at one in the morning.

15
00:00:30,335 --> 00:00:32,046
Shops stay open all night,

16
00:00:32,086 --> 00:00:34,605
and the owls start a support group for

17
00:00:34,645 --> 00:00:36,289
people who now work days.

18
00:00:37,450 --> 00:00:39,545
But the nights stay too short.

19
00:00:39,585 --> 00:00:41,823
Without darkness, nobody sleeps.

20
00:00:41,863 --> 00:00:43,247
People nap in cafes,

21
00:00:43,287 --> 00:00:44,884
on buses, and in trees.

22
00:00:44,924 --> 00:00:47,162
The city is bright and cheerful,

23
00:00:47,202 --> 00:00:50,365
but the whole place is also very, very yawny.

24
00:00:50,850 --> 00:00:52,013
Astronomers shrug.

25
00:00:52,053 --> 00:00:54,753
A real second sun would roast the planet,

26
00:00:54,793 --> 00:00:56,759
so in this thought experiment,

27
00:00:56,799 --> 00:00:58,430
it's a cool, gentle star,

28
00:00:58,470 --> 00:01:01,571
more like a friendly nightlight than a furnace.

29
00:01:02,050 --> 00:01:04,263
Everyone gets double shadows, too.

30
00:01:04,303 --> 00:01:06,517
One slides left, one slides right,

31
00:01:06,557 --> 00:01:10,030
and they cross like scissors every noon and midnight.

32
00:01:10,070 --> 00:01:13,012
Kids spend whole afternoons trying to step on

33
00:01:13,052 --> 00:01:13,874
both at once.

34
00:01:14,350 --> 00:01:15,433
Here's the twist.

35
00:01:15,473 --> 00:01:18,077
The second sun doesn't rise every night.

36
00:01:18,117 --> 00:01:19,861
It only rises when someone,

37
00:01:19,901 --> 00:01:21,843
somewhere, really needs light.

38
00:01:21,883 --> 00:01:25,147
Tonight it's for a little girl afraid of the dark.

39
00:01:25,850 --> 00:01:27,416
So the second sun rises,

40
00:01:27,456 --> 00:01:30,829
soft and silver, and the girl falls asleep smiling.

41
00:01:30,869 --> 00:01:33,572
Somewhere a rooster mutters, wrong again.

42
00:01:33,612 --> 00:01:35,714
And the whole sleepy town sighs:

43
00:01:35,754 --> 00:01:38,056
we'll see you tomorrow, little sun.
